WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.825
Child processes often inherit environment variables from the parent
process unless the application deliberately controls that behavior.

2
00:00:07.825 --> 00:00:17.558
This can expose credentials, tokens, connection strings, proxy settings,
path variables, debug flags, cloud configuration, or feature flags to code
that does not need them.

3
00:00:17.558 --> 00:00:24.923
Even when the child process is trusted, minimizing inherited environment
reduces blast radius and makes behavior easier to review.

4
00:00:24.923 --> 00:00:28.014
A safer pattern is explicit environment construction.

5
00:00:28.014 --> 00:00:34.458
The application decides which variables the child process needs for the
approved task and passes only those values.

6
00:00:34.458 --> 00:00:43.401
If a secret is required, the design should consider whether it can be
provided through a safer mechanism than a broad inherited environment or a
command argument.

7
00:00:43.401 --> 00:00:50.503
Secrets should not appear in logs, user-facing errors, process listings,
shell history, crash output, or monitoring records.

8
00:00:50.503 --> 00:00:53.265
Arguments are not always a safe place for secrets.

9
00:00:53.265 --> 00:01:03.261
Depending on the platform and tooling, command arguments may be visible in
diagnostics, process inspection, crash reports, job metadata, audit logs,
or operational dashboards.

10
00:01:03.261 --> 00:01:06.417
Environment variables can have similar exposure risks.

11
00:01:06.417 --> 00:01:14.769
The stable principle is to provide the least secret material necessary,
through the narrowest appropriate channel, for the shortest practical
time.

12
00:01:14.769 --> 00:01:17.399
Path-related environment values also matter.

13
00:01:17.399 --> 00:01:27.329
Variables that influence executable lookup, library loading, configuration
discovery, proxy behavior, or temporary locations can change what a child
process runs or reaches.

14
00:01:27.329 --> 00:01:31.669
The application should avoid letting untrusted input shape those
variables.

15
00:01:31.669 --> 00:01:39.560
Safe process execution includes controlling the environment as part of the
command contract, not treating it as invisible background state.
